Zero down time deployment strategies:
i.Rolling updates
“Zero-downtime deployment means releasing updates without affecting users. We follow deployment strategies like blue-green, canary, or rolling updates.
For example, in Kubernetes, I use rolling updates to gradually replace pods, so most of the service remains live while the new version rolls out.

Next, I manage traffic carefully. Load balancers and service meshes route requests only to healthy instances, ensuring users don’t face errors if
something goes wrong. I continuously monitor the system using tools like Prometheus and Grafana to track latency, errors, and performance.

In CI/CD pipelines, we handle builds, deployments, health checks, and automated rollbacks, so if an issue arises, we can revert to the stable

version quickly without manual intervention.
___________________-
ii.Canary:
Canary Deployment releases a new version of an application to a small subset of users or pods first.
The rest of the users continue using the stable old version while we monitor key metrics like  error rate, latency, 
and CPU usage are closely monitored before increasing traffic to the new version..
If no issues are detected, the new version is gradually rolled out to more users in phases.
This strategy reduces deployment risk, ensures smoother releases, and allows early detection of production issues.

iii.Blue green Deployment:
Blue-Green Deployment is a strategy where two identical environments—Blue and Green—run simultaneously.
One environment serves production traffic while the other remains idle.
When the new version is deployed and tested in the idle environment, traffic is switched from the active environment to the idle one.
This approach ensures minimal downtime, quick rollback, and safer production releases.
___________________________________
summary:
In production, Canary Deployment gradually releases a new version to a small subset of users, allowing early detection of issues with
minimal risk.
Blue-Green Deployment maintains two identical environments and switches traffic instantly to the new version, providing near-zero downtime and
very low risk.
Rolling Update replaces old instances or pods in batches, ensuring continuous availability but with moderate risk if any batch fails.
Overall, Blue-Green is most effective for critical systems requiring zero downtime, Canary is ideal for testing features safely, and 
Rolling Updates work well for standard routine updates.
